{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>And HaShem spoke to Moses.</b> The reason for the change in this parashah, in that it says <b>וַיֹּאמֶר</b> ('and He said'), unlike the entire Torah, where in all of them it says ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ ('and He spoke'), is explained in light of their statement, of blessed memory (Yalkut Shimoni), that when Moses saw the incident of the wood-gatherer, he said before HaShem: On weekdays Israel don tefillin and remember the commandments; on the Sabbath day, by what shall they remember? And the Holy One, blessed be He, replied: I shall give them the commandment of tzitzit, through which they shall remember, etc. — thus far. Therefore it says ״וַיֹּאמֶר״ — meaning, a saying that consoles the heart with what was sought."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:48.303733576Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}